{"success": true, "data": {"stdout": "{"text": "The Japanese word for \"handbag\" is a translation of the English word \"handbag\", \"handkerchief\". This word is widely used and well understood in Japan (and elsewhere around the world) even today.\n\nThe word \"baguette\" is also used as the English word for a typical French kitchen bag.\n\nOrigins\nJapanese words used for \"bag\" are a mix of Japanese and Western influences. Most people use \"bag\" for \"a bag\" and \"bag\" for \"handbag\", \"wand\" (a Japanese wand), \"washi\" (a Japanese towel) and \"shashimi\".\n\nA few Japanese people choose to use the English word for a bag (and the English word for \"bag\") to differentiate themselves from Japanese people who are unaware of the meaning of \"baguette\" or \"baguette\" and use them interchangeably. This is called \"bagjumping\" in Japanese.\n\nJapanese and Chinese people have traditionally distinguished between a \"bag\" and \"handbag\", although they often use the English word \"handbag\" or \"wand\" interchangeably, both with and without the definite article (\"the\").\n\nThe Japanese word for \"bag\" is \"baguette\" and the Chinese word for \"bag\" is \"handbag\" (北金探偵; wàn fú, literally \"two-handled handbag\", wàn síi, literally \"handbag and shoulder bag\" or wàn ōuán, literally \"two-handled shoulder bag\"). In fact, even Japanese \"baguette\" could be defined as 帝手料 (bāguette) or  (baguette) in the sense of \"two-handled\".\n\nThe \"two-handled\" is a translation of the French word \"baguette\", used by the French to define a \"baguette\" or \"French handbag\", where it means \"handbag with two handles\" or \"two-handled French handbag\", where the two handles are separated by a strip of cloth.\n\nIn Chinese, the \"two-handled\" is a translation of 川手料 (yífùshòu) or 冷手料 (yífùshòu, literally \"two-handled\"), and 李手料 (gu", "depth": 1, "seed_id": 0, "token_count": 512}
{"text": "This is an essay I wrote in February 2013 about the evolution of the concept of political correctness in the US. It was inspired by my own research on political correctness and intersectionality. For the purpose of this essay I chose to focus on the history of political correctness in the US and to avoid looking at any current debates concerning the term.\n\nIt follows an excerpt from another essay:\n\nIn 1996 the American Psychiatric Association (APA) published the manual for diagnosing psychiatric disorders in which sexual orientation and gender identity were removed from the list of mental disorders.\n\nIn June 1996 the APA released the latest edition of the Diagnostic and Statistical Manual of Mental Disorders (DSM), which removed homosexuality as a mental disorder, and included gender identity disorder as a mental illness instead. Both revisions were approved by the APA's Commission on Sexuality, Gender, and HIV/AIDS.\n\nIn March of 1997 the APA Board of Trustees approved a resolution in which they \"declined to consider a request for consideration that would have affirmed homosexuality as a mental illness\" in the DSM-V. According to one of the LGBT activists who spearheaded the issue and who has also written extensively about the history of political correctness, \"it was widely assumed that the APA had already decided to remove homosexuality as a mental illness in a resolution in 1997.\" However, as he stated, there were \"many members of the lesbian, gay, bisexual, transgender, queer community [that] believe that they were targeted.\"\n\nIn October 1997, in response to growing opposition to the revision, the APA issued a statement:\n\"The APA believes that homosexuality should not be classified as a mental disorder in the Diagnostic and Statistical Manual of Mental Disorders (DSM), fifth edition (DSM-5).\"\n\nThe following May, the APA Board of Trustees formally voted not to reconsider the matter during an upcoming meeting.\n\nIn addition, the US Department of Health and Human Services' Office of Civil Rights (OCR) began enforcing the 1992 Standards of Care with respect to sex and gender identity in June 1997; under the Standards, sex and gender identity were now considered psychiatric disorders. This policy, which did not change in 2000 with the publication of the first edition of the International Classification of Diseases and Related Health Problems, replaced the 1995 Standards, which had replaced the earlier 1991 Standards.\n\nThe change in the policy coincided with the rise of the Gay and Lesbian Rights Movement and in opposition to the American Psychiatric Association's 1996 decision", "depth": 1, "seed_id": 1, "token_count": 512}
{"text": "The Shinga are a people of western Ethiopia, one of the major clans in the Amhara Basin. They are the largest people in the area in which they live, and they constitute an important agricultural and pastoral culture. According to anthropologist Gérard Vigneau (1974) who studied the Haga people of Southern Ethiopia, the Shinga are the \"most ancient people in the area and represent an exceptionally ancient branch of a single lineage that has been around for some thousand years\". The Shinga speak a Semitic language closely related to the Amharic and the Aymara languages.\n\nThe Shinga live in a large central plain in Eastern Ethiopia that is bounded on the north by the Nile valley and on the east by the Omo River, while on the west the country's deserts and mountains are located. The Shinga are a largely nomadic people, though they can be found in towns, townships and kötas (rural areas). In the early centuries of the Common Era, the Shinga were the main kingdom of the region.\n\nThe Shinga live by their herds, mainly sheep. They are very pastoral, moving their herds with animals by the day and sleeping in camps by night. They practice long-distance trade through the Sudan, Djibouti and the Horn of Africa. They can be found in all of East Africa from southern Somalia to central Eritrea.\n\nShinga society is patriarchal, with a hereditary head of house and family. Each family has a female as head of family, the queen of the family (or of each male clan or area). Each Shinga house is headed by a king, and he typically comes from the clan of the first female head of house and from each male clan with the exception of the Amharic, Aymara, and Zagwe clans, which each have a king from their own respective clans. The sib of the Shinga are also known as a sib-jok, from an old term for head of the royal family. The family sib is the leader of the Shinga people, as well as of the Shinga nations and tribes across East Africa. The Shinga have a written language based on Proto-Djiboutian and is similar to the Amharic and Aymara languages.\n\nThe Shinga culture has been a central theme in ethnographic and anthropological studies, as it provides an invaluable", "depth": 1, "seed_id": 2, "token_count": 512}
