{"text": "TheIntroduction\nThe following is a transcript of the first part of a lecture delivered by Professor Paul Ehrlich to the American Association for the Advancement of Science's annual meeting, held in San Diego, California, in the fall of 1978.\n\nThe idea of an impending environmental catastrophe, and the need to make changes in our lifestyles, has been a major theme in American cultural life for several decades. This has become the dominant view and has been the basis for environmental activism on every continent. In this talk I will discuss, from a scientific viewpoint, the possibility of environmental catastrophe and the likely effects of any possible change in human behavior. I will present a number of reasons why the likelihood of environmental catastrophe is extremely small, and a few reasons why it is unlikely. The arguments are based on the best available science.\n\nEnvironmental problems are complex. The world's population, for example, has grown by a billion people over the last 100 years, yet that population has increased by only a tenth as fast as the planet's total mass. The United States and most of the rest of the world is not the same as it was in 1900. We are now a very different kind of human, with vastly different lifestyles and with vastly different environmental needs. The changes in our lifestyle are likely to be highly variable. Environmental problems arise because of the interaction of many factors, and scientists must be able to identify and analyze these factors to understand how environmental problems arise. A major task in this area is to analyze the consequences of various environmental trends, including population growth, population density, and consumption of natural resources. One of the most important environmental problems is that of the depletion of soil fertility. It is a serious problem in the developing world, where it can make a major contribution to food shortages. It is also a problem in the industrialized world, where it can make a major contribution to environmental pollution. The problem is so serious that it is often discussed in terms of the environment in general. It is sometimes referred to as the \"environmental crisis\". The term is more accurate because it describes not only problems of environmental degradation, but also of cultural degradation.\n\nWhen environmental problems are discussed in terms of ecology, the problem is often compared with the problem of population growth. The environmental problems we will discuss in this talk are, in some respects, more serious. The environmental problems to be discussed here are of such magnitude that they could lead to drastic environmental consequences.\n\nEnvironmental degradation is a major problem worldwide, but the problem is much larger in many parts", "depth": 2, "seed_id": 2, "token_count": 512}
