{"text": "This is a guest post from the team at Nesta, the UK's independent research and development agency.\n\nFor years, economists have debated the question of what it means to be an individual in a capitalist economy. One of the most common answers is that it is an individual's contribution to the production of wealth, measured by the market price of that wealth. In the 1980s, however, many economists began to question whether this \"economic individual\" concept captures an important dimension of human dignity. In particular, they argued that it does not adequately capture the concept of \"social equality\" or the idea that all individuals deserve equal access to opportunities, wealth and other resources. In this essay, we argue that the concept of \"economic individual\" is not a viable approach to understand human dignity, and instead argue that the concept of a \"human community\" or \"human community economy\" is more appropriate to help explain human dignity.\n\nIn this article we introduce the concept of a \"human community economy\" and demonstrate how this concept helps explain many of the major ethical dilemmas in our society today. We argue that human communities are formed by a variety of social and economic relationships, including the relationships between individuals, families and communities, with the latter being crucial to the functioning of any community. These relationships are reflected in the economic and political institutions that shape society and shape the way we relate to each other, including the way we define and understand our community. In this essay, we explore three key features of a human community economy:\n\nThe social and economic roles of individuals: a \"social individual\" is one who contributes to the common good through their own actions and/or actions of others; a \"social individual\" is also a member of a community;\n The role of institutions in forming and maintaining communities: an economic individual is an agent that makes a profit or receives a benefit from doing so; but an institution may also create a community;\n The role of money: money is a means of exchange in a community, but money can also be an agent of social control, and a mechanism for ensuring the economic and political power of particular groups\n\nHuman communities are also shaped by economic institutions. These institutions have many forms, but one of the most important ways in which they shape communities is through their economic structure and their relationship to money.\n\nIn a capitalist economy, the market determines the allocation of resources and the distribution of the benefits from those resources. In a human community, however, this decision-making process is shaped by", "depth": 3, "seed_id": 1, "token_count": 512}
{"text": "A former University of Missouri student has announced he's running for Missouri's 6th Congressional District seat.\n\nBen Jealous announced on his campaign's Facebook page that he's running for the seat currently held by Republican incumbent Peter Roskam.\n\n\"After much thought and prayer, I am announcing my candidacy for the 6th Congressional District of Missouri,\" Jealous wrote. \"I am running to elect progressives in Congress who will fight for a better America and for progressive values, including: raising the minimum wage, combating climate change, protecting reproductive rights, and investing in our children and our future. As a progressive, I believe that we must make our government work for all of us.\"\n\nAccording to the St. Louis Post-Dispatch, Jealous is the former president of the NAACP and the head of the NAACP's Washington office. He has been a major player in Democratic politics for several years, but his political rise has been slow, particularly in comparison with other national figures like Sen. Bernie Sanders.\n\n\"The Democratic Party has been taken over by the left,\" Jealous told the Washington Post. \"They have taken over the Democratic Party from being a party of the people, where people care about each other and their neighbors, and they have taken over the Democratic Party from being a party that fights for working people.\"\n\nJealous has had a long history of involvement in politics. He graduated from Harvard Law School and served as president of the NAACP. He ran for president in 2004, but was eliminated in the Iowa caucuses.\n\nJealous is the third Democratic candidate to enter the race, joining state Sen. Maria Chappelle-Nadal and former state Rep. Jason Holsman. Chappelle-Nadal was expected to be the Democratic frontrunner until she dropped out of the race in October.\n\n\"We need a Congress that fights for working families, not just Wall Street bankers,\" Jealous said. \"I believe I can build on the successes we have seen under President Obama, build on the success of Sen. Sanders, and build on the success of Rep. Ellison. We need a Congress that fights for working families.\"\n\nWhile Jealous was a member of the Democratic National Committee, he was an independent and didn't endorse any of the presidential candidates.\n\nRoskam is currently serving his seventh term as the congressman from the 6th district. He's been involved in politics since he was elected in 2000. He won re-election in 2006, 2008, and 2012.", "depth": 3, "seed_id": 2, "token_count": 512}
